Ordering a window is not moving it, and saying otherwise destroyed a window mid call.

-[NSWindow orderWindow:relativeTo:] posted NSWindowWillMoveNotification on EVERY ordering. The
comment above it said the real conditions were unknown and it was done all the time until they were
figured out. They are figured out: AppKit posts it when the frame origin is about to change, which
ordering does not do, and platformWindowWillMove already posts it when the compositor really moves
the window. That is the only producer left.

The invented notification is delivered SYNCHRONOUSLY, so it ran arbitrary application code in the
middle of whatever asked for the ordering. Qt closes a popup when its window moves, which is correct
popup behaviour, so showing the completer in CMake ran

  QCompleter::complete -> QWidgetPrivate::setVisible -> QWindowPrivate::setVisible ->
  QCocoaWindow::setVisible -> our orderWindow: -> this notification -> Qt DESTROYING the popup
  platform window

and QCocoaWindow::setVisible then carried on using the object that had just been deleted underneath
it. It faulted 1805 bytes later reading the QWindow out of freed memory, a GENERAL PROTECTION fault
on a non canonical pointer, which is why CR2 was 0 and said nothing.

HOW IT WAS PINNED DOWN, because the reasoning went wrong twice before this. QNSWindow and QNSPanel
hold their QCocoaWindow in a QPointer, so asking a QNS window for -platformWindow answers NULL
exactly when the C++ object has been destroyed. Bracketing this single notification with that
question printed the object healthy before the post and gone after it. Before that, the function had
to be identified from __unwind_info rather than from symbols: libqcocoa is stripped and every frame
resolves to qt_plugin_instance, and reading register roles out of a disassembly started at the wrong
address produced two confident and wrong conclusions.

MEASURED: typing a path into the source field crashed on 100 percent of runs before and on 0 of 3
after, with the path visible in the field at 142, 159, 159 ink and still correct after the resize.

WITH THIS CMAKE MEETS ALL THREE CRITERIA: it renders its whole interface, it takes mouse and
keyboard and shows what is typed, and it relayouts on a compositor resize.
